Subject: Doubt regarding estimation of standard error
Message-ID: <yEoqlDnV8zM.Dp_CDZPz7QsJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/yEoqlDnV8zM/m/Dp_CDZPz7QsJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hello Phil,

I got two part question here. First, For dichotomous and polytomous data which method of standard error estimates between 'crossprod'  and 'complete' is better as far as precision  is concerned?

Second, I was using 'crossprod' for dichotomous  data under 3PL model.

mirt(data,1, itemtype ="3PL" ,TOL=0.01, SE=TRUE)

Iteration: 60, Log-Lik: -211043.855, Max-Change: 0.00312

Calculating information matrix...

Warning message:

In loadESTIMATEinfo(info = tmp, ESTIMATE = ESTIMATE, constrain = constrain,  :

  Could not invert information matrix; model likely is not identified.

I got above warning message and stand error were not shown.

whereas when I used 'complete' method on the same data, standard errors were shown and no warning messages were displayed.

why is that?

Thanking you,

Irshad

From ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com  Wed Jun 10 15:45:20 2015
From: ub330c2028fed415b at groups.google.com (Phil Chalmers)
Date: Wed, 10 Jun 2015 15:45:20 +0000
Subject: Re: Doubt regarding estimation of standard error
Message-ID: <yEoqlDnV8zM.KptrLv7XNoAJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <yEoqlDnV8zM.Dp_CDZPz7QsJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
References: <yEoqlDnV8zM.Dp_CDZPz7QsJ@mirt-package.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/mirt-package/c/yEoqlDnV8zM/m/KptrLv7XNoAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

You never want complete, it is the information matrix matrix from the complete data solution (which is overly conservative, because it assumes you know the values of theta...which you don't). You probably want 'Louis' instead, which should be more accurate since its the actual observed information matrix (the crossprod is an approximation to this, but its very cheap to compute).

That being said, good luck getting a positive definite information matrix for the 3PL model, You sample size must be *very* large for this to occur, which is one of the downsides of this model. Cheers.

Phil

